  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Sunday)\nIssued at 1059 AM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nCurrent upper-air analysis and water vapor imagery depict synoptic-\nscale ridging continuing to build across central CONUS and into the \nCWA, meanwhile synoptic-scale troughing is positioned off the \nPacific coast. Satellite imagery indicates scattered mid and high \nlevel clouds across the region. Surface observations show \ntemperatures mainly in the upper 40s and 50s, with dewpoints mainly \nin the 20s to 30s. Winds are currently southwesterly across much of \nthe southern half of the CWA, with gusts up to ~30kts, while \nlighter, variable winds are present across the northern half of the \nCWA. \n\nToday will see temperatures roughly 10-15 degrees above average as \nridging continues to build over the region. Patchy fog will be \npossible again tonight across much of the South Dakota plains. This \ntrend will continue into Tuesday as the ridge continues to slide \neastward. However, the pattern will begin to shift Tuesday evening \ninto Wednesday as a surface low moves across Wyoming and into the \nregion. This system will bring chances for light precipitation across \nnortheast Wyoming, the northern Black Hills, and northwestern South \nDakota. Most locations are expected to see less than 0.10\" with a \nslight chance of seeing 0.20-0.30\" on the high end. There will be a \nchance for snow mixing in with rain, however, little to no \naccumulations are expected. Gusty, southeasterly winds (up to \n~45kts) are expected to develop Tuesday night across portions of \nnortheast Wyoming ahead of the frontal passage on Wednesday. With \nthe frontal passage Wednesday afternoon into evening, winds are \nexpected to shift to the west/northwest with gusts as high as 50-\n55kts possible. No wind headlines have been drawn up yet, but a Wind \nAdvisory will most likely be issued for portions of the South Dakota \nplains. Temperatures behind the front will be much cooler, with \nhighs in the 30s for the northern half of the CWA to low 50s in the \nsouthern half.\n\nThe coastal trough is still expected to push inland and bring the \nnext system Friday into Saturday, offering a better shot at \nwidespread precipitation, a continued cooldown, and a growing \npotential for snow. QPF and snowfall totals have been trending \nlower, however, it is still to far out to nail down exact ranges. \nTemperatures are expected to stay on the cooler, seasonal side as \nthis system pushes through and the days following it.",
